<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suiyuan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1928">1928</a>–<a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suiyuan was a province of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in what is now central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, with its capital at Guisui, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohhot">Hohhot</a>. Formed in 1928, it was largely occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1937 and incorporated into Mengjiang. The People's Republic merged it into the Inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> Autonomous Region in 1954.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suiyuan.jpg" alt="Suiyuan" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lilauid · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Republic_of_China_edcp_location_map_(disputed_territories)_Suiy%C3%BCan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
